@noindent
The environment is crucial to the evaluation process, because it determines the
context in which an expression should be evaluated.  Indeed, one could say that
expressions in a programming language do not, in themselves, have any meaning.
Rather, an expression acquires a meaning only with respect to some environment
in which it is evaluated.  Even the interpretation of an expression as
straightforward as @code{1L + 1L} depends on an understanding that one is
operating in a context in which @code{+} is the operator for addition.  Thus, in
our model of evaluation we will always speak of evaluating an expression with
respect to some environment.  To describe programs, we will suppose that there
is a global environment, consisting of a single frame (with no enclosing
environment) that holds every top-level declaration, every function and
@code{val} declared directly in a file, not nested inside another
declaration.  Kotlin resolves these names when the program is compiled, so the
global environment of a running Kotlin program is fixed before the program
starts; the frames this chapter is about are the ones a function call or a
lambda opens while the program runs, and they hold parameters, local
declarations, and captured variables.  The idea that @code{+} is the operator
for addition needs no binding at all: it is part of the language itself,
outside every frame.  Chapter 4's evaluator gives this model a concrete data
structure: a chain of frames, each a set of bindings from name to value plus a
link to the frame it extends. Every captured @code{var} in this
section is what that structure realizes.

