@node	3.2.1, 3.2.2, 3.2, 3.2
@subsection The Rules for Evaluation

The overall specification of how a call is evaluated
remains the same as when we first introduced it in @ref{1.1.3}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
To evaluate a call:

@end itemize

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the function expression and every argument expression.@footnote{Assignment introduces a
subtlety into step 1 of the evaluation rule.  As shown in @ref{Exercise 3.8},
the presence of assignment allows us to write expressions that will produce
different values depending on the order in which the subexpressions of a call
are evaluated.  Kotlin closes the gap this footnote originally opened: a
call's receiver and argument expressions are always evaluated left to right,
a guarantee of the language rather than an implementation choice, so
@ref{Exercise 3.8} asks you to build the probe that would have been needed to
discover an unspecified order, and to confirm that Kotlin leaves nothing left
to discover.}

@item
Apply the value of the function expression to the values of the argument
expressions.

@end enumerate

@noindent
The environment model of evaluation replaces the substitution model in
specifying what it means to apply a function to arguments.

In the environment model of evaluation, a function value is always a pair
consisting of some code and a pointer to an environment.  Function values are
created in one way only: by evaluating a lambda expression.  This produces a
function value whose code is obtained from the text of the lambda expression
and whose environment is the environment in which the lambda expression was
evaluated to produce the function value.  For example, consider the function
declaration

@example kotlin
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x
@end example

@noindent
evaluated in the global environment.  The @code{fun} declaration syntax is just
syntactic sugar for an underlying implicit lambda expression.  It would
have been equivalent to have used

@example kotlin
val squareFn: (Long) -> Long = @{ x -> x * x @}
@end example

@noindent
which evaluates @code{@{ x -> x * x @}} and binds @code{squareFn} to the
resulting value, all in the global environment under a fresh name, since
Kotlin refuses a second declaration of @code{square} in one scope.

@ref{Figure 3.2} shows the result of evaluating this @code{fun} declaration.
The function object is a pair whose code specifies that the function has one
formal parameter, namely @code{x}, and a function body @code{x * x}.  The
environment part of the function is a pointer to the global environment, since
that is the environment in which the lambda expression was evaluated to
produce the function.  A new binding, which associates the function object
with the name @code{square}, has been added to the global frame.  In general,
a top-level declaration creates a binding by adding it to a frame.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.2}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.2:} Environment structure produced by
evaluating @code{fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x} in the global environment.

@example
           +----------------------+
           | other variables      |
global --->|                      |
env        | square: --+          |
           +-----------|----------+
                       |       ^
fun square(x: Long):  |       |
Long = x * x          V       |
                   .---.---.   |
                   | O | O-+---+
                   `-|-^---'
                     |
                     V
                   parameters: x
                   body: x * x
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.2b,,81mm,Figure 3.2: Environment structure produced by evaluating fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
