@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.2:} Environment structure produced by evaluating @code{fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
Now that we have seen how functions are created, we can describe how
functions are applied.  The environment model specifies: To apply a function
to arguments, create a new environment containing a frame that binds the
parameters to the values of the arguments.  The enclosing environment of this
frame is the environment specified by the function.  Now, within this new
environment, evaluate the function body.

To show how this rule is followed, @ref{Figure 3.3} illustrates the environment
structure created by evaluating the expression @code{square(5L)} in the global
environment, where @code{square} is the function generated in @ref{Figure 3.2}.
Applying the function results in the creation of a new environment,
labeled E1 in the figure, that begins with a frame in which @code{x}, the
formal parameter for the function, is bound to the argument 5.  The pointer
leading upward from this frame shows that the frame's enclosing environment is
the global environment.  The global environment is chosen here, because this is
the environment that is indicated as part of the @code{square} function
object.  Within E1, we evaluate the body of the function, @code{x * x}.
Since the value of @code{x} in E1 is 5, the result is @code{5 * 5}, or 25.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.3}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.3:} Environment created by evaluating
@code{square(5L)} in the global environment.

@example
