@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.3b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.3:} Environment created by evaluating @code{square(5L)} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
The environment model of function application can be summarized by two rules:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A function object is applied to a set of arguments by constructing a frame,
binding the formal parameters of the function to the arguments of the call,
and then evaluating the body of the function in the context of the new
environment constructed.  The new frame has as its enclosing environment the
environment part of the function object being applied.

@item
A function is created by evaluating a lambda expression relative to a
given environment.  The resulting function object is a pair consisting of the
text of the lambda expression and a pointer to the environment in which
the function was created.

@end itemize

@noindent
We also specify that declaring a name with @code{val} or @code{fun} creates a
binding in the current environment frame and assigns to the name the indicated
value.@footnote{Kotlin, unlike the original book's definition form, refuses a second
declaration of the same name in the same frame outright: the compiler
reports a redeclaration error rather than silently changing the existing
binding.  Where the book's footnote here notes that some people prefer
redefinitions of existing symbols to signal errors, Kotlin simply takes that
side: the only way to change a name's value after it is declared is to have
declared it @code{var} in the first place and assign to it, which is exactly
the mechanism @ref{3.1} introduced.} Finally, we
specify the behavior of assignment to a captured @code{var}, the operation
that forced us to introduce the environment model in the first place.
Evaluating an assignment such as @code{@var{variable} = @var{value}} or
@code{@var{variable} -= @var{value}} in some environment locates the
binding of the variable in the environment and changes that binding to indicate
the new value.  That is, one finds the first frame in the environment that
contains a binding for the variable and modifies that frame.  Kotlin checks
this at compile time rather than at the moment of assignment: a name that
was declared @code{val}, or that has no visible declaration at all, is a
compile error, so the analogue of ``assignment signals an error'' for an
unbound variable can never happen at run time in Kotlin: the program that
would trigger it does not compile.

These evaluation rules, though considerably more complex than the substitution
model, are still reasonably straightforward.  Moreover, the evaluation model,
though abstract, provides a correct description of how the interpreter
evaluates expressions.  In @ref{Chapter 4} we shall see how this model can
serve as a blueprint for implementing a working interpreter: the evaluator's
environment structure, a frame of bindings plus a link to the frame it extends,
is that blueprint made concrete.  The following
sections elaborate the details of the model by analyzing some illustrative
programs.

@node	3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.2.1, 3.2
@subsection Applying Simple Procedures

When we introduced the substitution model in @ref{1.1.5} we showed how
the call @code{f(5L)} evaluates to 136, given the following function
declarations:

@example kotlin
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x

fun sumOfSquares(x: Long, y: Long): Long =
    square(x) + square(y)

fun f(a: Long): Long = sumOfSquares(a + 1, a * 2)
@end example

@noindent
We can analyze the same example using the environment model.  @ref{Figure 3.4}
shows the three function objects created by evaluating the declarations of
@code{f}, @code{square}, and @code{sumOfSquares} in the global environment.
Each function object consists of some code, together with a pointer to the
global environment.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.4}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.4:} Procedure objects in the global frame.

@example
