        |      |       | y: 10 |        |      |        |       |
        +------+       +-------+        +------+        +-------+
   sumOfSquares(     square(x) +       x * x           x * x
     a + 1,            square(y))
     a * 2)
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.5b,,142mm,Figure 3.5: Environments created by evaluating f(5L) using the procedures in Figure 3.4.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.5b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.5:} Environments created by evaluating @code{f(5L)} @w{using} the procedures in @ref{Figure 3.4}.}
@end float

@noindent
To evaluate this call, we first evaluate the subexpressions.  The first
subexpression, @code{sumOfSquares}, has a value that is a function object.
(Notice how this value is found: We first look in the first frame of E1, which
contains no binding for @code{sumOfSquares}.  Then we proceed to the
enclosing environment, i.e. the global environment, and find the binding shown
in @ref{Figure 3.4}.)  The other two subexpressions are evaluated, left to
right, by applying the operators @code{+} and @code{*} to evaluate @code{a +
1} and @code{a * 2}, obtaining 6 and 10, respectively.

@noindent
Now we apply the function object @code{sumOfSquares} to the arguments 6 and
10.  This results in a new environment E2 in which the formal parameters
@code{x} and @code{y} are bound to the arguments.  Within E2 we evaluate the
expression @code{square(x) + square(y)}.  This leads us to evaluate
@code{square(x)}, where @code{square} is found in the global frame and
@code{x} is 6.  Once again, we set up a new environment, E3, in which @code{x}
is bound to 6, and within this we evaluate the body of @code{square}, which is
@code{x * x}.  Also as part of applying @code{sumOfSquares}, we must
evaluate the subexpression @code{square(y)}, where @code{y} is 10.  This
second call to @code{square} creates another environment, E4, in which
@code{x}, the formal parameter of @code{square}, is bound to 10.  And within E4
we must evaluate @code{x * x}.

The important point to observe is that each call to @code{square} creates a new
environment containing a binding for @code{x}.  We can see here how the
different frames serve to keep separate the different local variables all named
@code{x}.  Notice that each frame created by @code{square} points to the global
environment, since this is the environment indicated by the @code{square}
function object.

After the subexpressions are evaluated, the results are returned.  The values
generated by the two calls to @code{square} are added by @code{sumOfSquares},
and this result is returned by @code{f}.  Since our focus here is on the
environment structures, we will not dwell on how these returned values are
passed from call to call; however, this is also an important aspect of the
evaluation process, and we will return to it in detail in @ref{Chapter 5}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.9}Exercise 3.9:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 3.9 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.} In @ref{1.2.1} we used the
substitution model to analyze two functions for computing factorials, a
recursive version

@example kotlin
fun factorial(n: Long): Long =
    if (n <= 1L) 1L else n * factorial(n - 1L)
@end example

@noindent
and an iterative version

@example kotlin
tailrec fun factIter(
    product: Long,
    counter: Long,
    maxCount: Long,
): Long =
    if (counter > maxCount) @{
        product
    @} else @{
        factIter(
            counter * product,
            counter + 1,
            maxCount,
        )
    @}

fun factorialIter(n: Long): Long = factIter(1L, 1L, n)
@end example

Draw the stack of frames live at the deepest point of evaluating
@code{factorial(6L)}, and show that @code{factorialIter(6L)} never has more
than one frame live at a time.@footnote{The environment
model will not clarify our claim in @ref{1.2.1} that the interpreter
can execute a function such as @code{factIter} in a constant amount of space
using tail recursion.  Kotlin restores that guarantee only for a function
marked @code{tailrec}: the compiler rewrites the one self-recursive call in
tail position as a loop, and refuses the mark (a build error under this
edition's @code{allWarningsAsErrors}) when no call is in tail position.  We
will discuss how an interpreter can supply the guarantee for every recursive
call, tail-marked or not, in @ref{5.4}.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 3.9a is added by this edition and extends exercise 3.9; SICP
numbers stop at 3.9.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.9a}Exercise 3.9a:} Exercise
3.9 asked you to draw the stack of frames each version of @code{factorial}
builds; this exercise makes the difference measurable.  Write functions
@code{runRecursive} and @code{runTailrec} that each run one of exercise 3.9's
two factorials on a thread whose stack is a caller-supplied number of bytes,
and report the outcome as a @code{StackOutcome}: @code{Overflow} when the run
dies with a @code{StackOverflowError}, @code{Completed} with the value when it
answers.  Then run each version with @code{n = 100000} on a 64 KiB stack, and
explain, in terms of frames and enclosing environments, what a
@code{StackOverflowError} says about the recursive version's structure, why no
@code{n} can produce that outcome for the @code{tailrec} version, and what
Kotlin checks before it accepts the @code{tailrec} mark.
@end quotation

@node	3.2.3, 3.2.4, 3.2.2, 3.2
@subsection Frames as the Repository of Local State

We can turn to the environment model to see how functions and assignment can
be used to represent objects with local state.  As an example, consider the
``withdrawal processor'' from @ref{3.1.1} created by calling the
function

@example kotlin
fun makeWithdraw(balance: Long): (Long) -> Either<WithdrawError,
    Long> @{
    var b = balance
    return @{ amount ->
        if (amount > b) @{
            Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
        @} else @{
            b -= amount
            Either.Right(b)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Let us describe the evaluation of

@example kotlin
val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L)
@end example

@noindent
followed by

@example kotlin
w1(50L)
// => Either.Right(50)
@end example

@noindent
@ref{Figure 3.6} shows the result of declaring the @code{makeWithdraw}
function in the global environment.  This produces a function object that
contains a pointer to the global environment.  So far, this is no different
from the examples we have already seen, except that the body of the function
is itself a lambda expression.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.6}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{makeWithdraw} in the global environment.

@example
