global -->| makeWithdraw:  --+        |
env       +------------------|--------+
                             |      ^
                             V      |
                         .---.---.  |
                         | O | O-+--+
                         `-|-^---'
                           |
                           V
         parameters: balance
         body: var b = balance
               return @{ amount ->
                   if (amount > b)
                       Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
                   else @{
                       b -= amount
                       Either.Right(b)
                   @}
               @}
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.6c,,128mm,Figure 3.6: Result of defining makeWithdraw in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.6c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{makeWithdraw} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
The interesting part of the computation happens when we apply the function
@code{makeWithdraw} to an argument:

@example kotlin
val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L)
@end example

@noindent
We begin, as usual, by setting up an environment E1 in which the immutable
parameter @code{balance} is bound to the argument 100.  The body first copies
that value into the mutable local @code{b}.  Evaluating the lambda then
constructs a function object that captures @code{b}.  Its code is the lambda
body, and its environment is E1.
The resulting function object is the value returned by the call to
@code{makeWithdraw}.  This is bound to @code{w1} in the global environment,
since the @code{val} declaration itself is being evaluated in the global
environment.  @ref{Figure 3.7} shows the resulting environment structure.

The shared artwork in Figures 3.7--3.10 labels the stored account state
@code{balance}.  In this Kotlin implementation, that label denotes the captured
@code{b} cell, not the immutable parameter.  The artwork omits the parameter
after initialization; the text diagrams show both bindings.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.7}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L)}.

@example
