          | makeWithdraw: ------------------------+       |
global -->|                                       |       |
          | w1: --+                               |       |
          +-------|-------------------------------|-------+
                  |                ^              |     ^
                  |                |              V     |
                  |        +-------+------+   .---.---. |
                  |  E1 -->| balance: 100 |   | O | O-+-+
                  |        | b: 100       |   `-|-^---'
                  |        +--------------+        |
                  V                ^               V
              .---.---.            |         parameters: balance
            +-+-O | O-+------------+         body: ...
            | `---^---'
            V
    parameters: amount
    body: if (amount > b)
              Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
          else @{
              b -= amount
              Either.Right(b)
          @}
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.7b,,145mm,Figure 3.7: Result of evaluating val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L).,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.7b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L)}.}
@end float

@noindent
Now we can analyze what happens when @code{w1} is applied to an argument:

@example kotlin
w1(50L)
// => Either.Right(50)
@end example

@noindent
We begin by constructing a frame in which @code{amount}, the formal parameter
of @code{w1}, is bound to the argument 50.  The crucial point to observe is
that this frame has as its enclosing environment not the global environment,
but rather the environment E1, because this is the environment that is
specified by the @code{w1} function object.  Within this new environment, we
evaluate the body of the function:

@example kotlin
if (amount > b) @{
    Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
@} else @{
    b -= amount
    Either.Right(b)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The resulting environment structure is shown in @ref{Figure 3.8}.  The
expression being evaluated references both @code{amount} and @code{b}.
@code{Amount} will be found in the first frame in the environment, while
@code{b} will be found by following the enclosing-environment pointer to
E1.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.8}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the procedure object @code{w1}.

@example
          +---------------------------------------------------+
          | makeWithdraw: ...                                 |
global -->|                                                   |
env       | w1: --+                                           |
          +-------|-------------------------------------------+
                  |               ^
                  |               |
                  |       +-------+------+ Here is the cell
                  | E1 -->| balance: 100 | that will be changed
                  |       | b: 100       | by the assignment.
                  |       +--------------+
                  V               ^   ^
              .---.---.           |   +----+
              | O | O-+-----------+        |
              `-|-^---'             +------+-----+
                |                   | amount: 50 |
                V                   +------------+
      parameters: amount   if (amount > b)
      body: ...                Either.Left(WithdrawError.
                                   InsufficientFunds)
                           else @{
                               b -= amount
                               Either.Right(b)
                           @}
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.8c,,145mm,Figure 3.8: Environments created by applying the procedure object w1.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.8c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the procedure object @code{w1}.}
@end float

@noindent
When the assignment @code{b -= amount} is executed, the binding of @code{b} in
E1 is changed.  At the completion of the call to @code{w1}, @code{b} is 50,
and the frame that contains @code{b} is still pointed to by the function
object @code{w1}.  The frame that binds @code{amount} (in which we executed
the code that changed @code{b}) is no longer relevant, since the call
that constructed it has terminated, and there are no pointers to that
frame from other parts of the environment.  The next time @code{w1} is called,
this will build a new frame that binds @code{amount} and whose enclosing
environment is E1.  We see that E1 serves as the ``place'' that holds the local
state variable for the function object @code{w1}.  @ref{Figure 3.9} shows the
situation after the call to @code{w1}.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.9}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.9:} Environments after the call to @code{w1}.

@example
