@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.10}Exercise 3.10:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 3.10 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.} In the @code{makeWithdraw}
function, the local variable @code{balance} is created as a parameter of
@code{makeWithdraw}.  We could also create the local state variable
explicitly, as a local declaration:

@example kotlin
fun makeWithdrawLet(initialAmount: Long): (Long) ->
    Either<WithdrawError, Long> @{
    var balance = initialAmount
    return @{ amount ->
        if (amount > balance) @{
            Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
        @} else @{
            balance -= amount
            Either.Right(balance)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

Recall from @ref{1.3.2} that the original book's local binding form is
syntactic sugar for a procedure call, which is why the book's alternate
version of
@code{makeWithdraw} builds one more frame than the version above.  A Kotlin
local @code{var} declaration is not a call at all, so it adds no frame of its
own; it binds in the frame the enclosing function call already built.

Use the environment model to analyze this alternate version of
@code{makeWithdraw}, drawing figures like the ones above to illustrate the
interactions

@example kotlin
val w1 = makeWithdrawLet(100L)

w1(50L)
// => Either.Right(50)

val w2 = makeWithdrawLet(100L)
@end example

Show that the two versions of @code{makeWithdraw} create objects with the same
behavior.  How do the environment structures differ for the two versions?
@end quotation

@node	3.2.4, 3.3, 3.1, 3.2
@subsection Internal Definitions

Section @ref{1.1.8} introduced the idea that functions can have internal
definitions, thus leading to a block structure as in the following function to
compute square roots:

@example kotlin
fun sqrt(x: Double): Double @{
    fun goodEnough(guess: Double): Boolean =
        abs(guess * guess - x) < 0.001

    fun improve(guess: Double): Double =
        (guess + x / guess) / 2.0
    tailrec fun sqrtIter(guess: Double): Double =
        if (goodEnough(guess)) guess else sqrtIter(improve(guess))

    return sqrtIter(1.0)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Now we can use the environment model to see why these internal definitions
behave as desired.  @ref{Figure 3.11} shows the point in the evaluation of the
expression @code{sqrt(2.0)} where the internal function @code{goodEnough} has
been called for the first time with @code{guess} equal to 1.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.11}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.

@example
          +--------------------------------------------------+
global -->| sqrt: --+                                        |
env       |         |                                        |
          +---------|----------------------------------------+
                    V       ^                   ^
                .---.---.   |                   |
     +----------+-O | O-+---+        +----------+------------+
     |          `---^---'            | x: 2                  |
     V                         E1 -->| goodEnough: -+       |
parameters: x                        | improve: ... |       |
body: fun goodEnough(guess) ...      | sqrtIter: ...|       |
      fun improve(guess) ...         +----------------|------+
      tailrec fun sqrtIter(guess) ... ^  ^            |     ^
      sqrtIter(1.0)                   |  |            V     |
                            +---------++ |        .---.---. |
                      E2 -->| guess: 1 | |        | O | O-+-+
                            +----------+ |        `-|-^---'
                      call to sqrtIter   |          |
                                         |          V
                               +---------++    parameters: guess
                         E3 -->| guess: 1 |    body: abs(guess *
                               +----------+      guess - x) < 0.001
                         call to goodEnough?
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.11b,,147mm,Figure 3.11: sqrt procedure with internal definitions.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.11b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.}
@end float

@noindent
Observe the structure of the environment.  @code{sqrt} is a name in the global
environment that is bound to a function object whose associated environment
is the global environment.  When @code{sqrt} was called, a new environment E1
was formed, subordinate to the global environment, in which the parameter
@code{x} is bound to 2.  The body of @code{sqrt} was then evaluated in E1.
Since the first declaration in the body of @code{sqrt} is

@example kotlin
fun goodEnough(guess: Double): Boolean = abs(guess * guess - x) < 0.001
@end example

@noindent
evaluating this declaration bound the function @code{goodEnough} in the
environment E1.  To be more precise, the name @code{goodEnough} was added to
the first frame of E1, bound to a function object whose associated
environment is E1.  Similarly, @code{improve} and @code{sqrtIter} were
declared as local functions in E1.  For conciseness, @ref{Figure 3.11} shows
only the function object for @code{goodEnough}.

Kotlin binds local declarations in the order they are written, so a local
function can call only the names declared above it in the same block --
@code{sqrtIter}, which calls both @code{goodEnough} and @code{improve}, is
declared last for exactly this reason.@footnote{This differs from the original book's
internal-definitions rule, which scans the whole body before evaluating any
of it, so an internal definition there may appear in any order and two
internal procedures may call each other.  A Kotlin local function that needs
to call one declared after it needs a different shape, such as a mutable
property holding a lambda, assigned after both are ready.}

@noindent
After the local declarations, the expression @code{sqrtIter(1.0)} was
evaluated, still in environment E1.  So the function object bound to
@code{sqrtIter} in E1 was called with 1.0 as an argument.  This created an
environment E2 in which @code{guess}, the parameter of @code{sqrtIter}, is
bound to 1.0.  @code{sqrtIter} in turn called @code{goodEnough} with the value
of @code{guess} (from E2) as the argument for @code{goodEnough}.  This set up
another environment, E3, in which @code{guess} (the parameter of
@code{goodEnough}) is bound to 1.0.  Although @code{sqrtIter} and
@code{goodEnough} both have a parameter named @code{guess}, these are two
distinct local variables located in different frames.  Also, E2 and E3 both
have E1 as their enclosing environment, because the @code{sqrtIter} and
@code{goodEnough} functions both have E1 as their environment part.  One
consequence of this is that the name @code{x} that appears in the body of
@code{goodEnough} will reference the binding of @code{x} that appears in E1,
namely the value of @code{x} with which the original @code{sqrt} function was
called.

The environment model thus explains the two key properties that make local
function declarations a useful technique for modularizing programs:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The names of the local functions do not interfere with names external to the
enclosing function, because the local function names will be bound in the
frame that the function creates when it is run, rather than being bound in
the global environment.

@item
The local functions can access the arguments of the enclosing function,
simply by using parameter names as free variables.  This is because the body
of the local function is evaluated in an environment that is subordinate to
the evaluation environment for the enclosing function.

@end itemize

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.11}Exercise 3.11:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 3.11 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.} In @ref{3.2.3} we saw how
the environment model described the behavior of functions with local state.
Now we have seen how internal definitions work.  A typical message-passing
object contains both of these aspects.  Consider the bank account function of
@ref{3.1.1}:

@example kotlin
interface BankAccount @{
    fun withdraw(amount: Long): Either<WithdrawError,
        Long>
    fun deposit(amount: Long): Long
@}

fun makeBankAccount(balance: Long): BankAccount @{
    var b = balance
    return object : BankAccount @{
        override fun withdraw(
            amount: Long,
        ): Either<WithdrawError, Long> =
            if (amount > b) @{
                Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
            @} else @{
                b -= amount
                Either.Right(b)
            @}

        override fun deposit(amount: Long): Long @{
            b += amount
            return b
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

Show the environment structure generated by the sequence of interactions

@example kotlin
val acc = makeBankAccount(50L)

acc.deposit(40L)
// => 90

acc.withdraw(60L)
// => Either.Right(30)
@end example

Where is the local state for @code{acc} kept?  Suppose we define another
account

@example kotlin
val acc2 = makeBankAccount(100L)
@end example

How are the local states for the two accounts kept distinct?  Which parts of
the environment structure are shared between @code{acc} and @code{acc2}?
@end quotation
